<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Triumph of Shapur I</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Triumph of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a> refers to rock reliefs carved in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> to commemorate the victories of the Sasanian king Shapur I over Roman emperors in the third century CE. The best known, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-rostam">Naqsh-e Rostam</a>, shows the captive emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valerian">Valerian</a> kneeling or submitting before the mounted king. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> treat these monuments as expressions of the dynasty's early military triumphs and royal ideology.</p></div>
